<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?>
<!--
  The Win32 application manifest embedded in slipcase-open.exe, and the only
  thing in it is the DPI declaration.

  WHY IT EXISTS

  The Windows App Certification Kit reported this application as not DPI aware
  on 2026-09-06, because there was no manifest here to read:

      DPIAwarenessValidation  WARNING
      The app Excelano.SlipcaseOpen_0.1.4.0_x64__nbxmgv0sk86m4 is not DPI Aware.

  WHAT IT ACTUALLY CHANGES, WHICH IS MORE THAN THE SIBLING'S DOES

  `slipcase-desktop` carries the same declaration and it is close to a formality
  there: `winit` sets per-monitor awareness at run time inside `EventLoop::new`,
  so the process was already aware and the kit was reading a file rather than
  the process. **This crate calls nothing of the kind.** Without this file the
  process really is unaware, and Windows bitmap-stretches what it draws — which
  on this product means the refusal dialog and the notification-area icon, and
  those two are the whole of what a person ever sees. So this is a behaviour
  change wearing the clothes of a declaration, and it is the right one: the
  alternative is a blurry message box on every high-DPI display.

  A declaration also takes effect before any of this program's code runs, where
  a run-time call would leave a window in which the process exists at the wrong
  awareness.

  WHY IT IS NOT A BUILD STEP

  The usual way to embed a manifest is `rc.exe` or `windres`, and this project
  has no resource compiler and wants none — `packaging/windows/README.md`
  rejected exactly that when the tray needed its icons, which is why those are
  loaded from files beside the binary at run time. `build.rs` hands the MSVC
  linker `/MANIFEST:EMBED` and `/MANIFESTINPUT` instead, and the linker that was
  already linking the binary embeds it. No resource compiler, no object file,
  nothing compiled that was not compiled before.

  WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT

  No `assemblyIdentity`: it would carry another version number in a project that
  keeps one number in two spellings, and nothing reads it.

  No `<compatibility>` or `supportedOS` block. Those change what Windows reports
  to the process about itself, which is a behaviour change with none of this
  one's justification, and nothing has asked for one.

  Both values, because different vintages of Windows read different ones and
  they must agree: `dpiAware` is what a pre-1703 reader understands and
  `dpiAwareness` is what everything since prefers. `PerMonitorV2` rather than
  system awareness because V2 is the one that scales dialogs and common controls
  for us — and a dialog is this application's only drawn surface, so the thing
  V2 adds is precisely the thing this needs.

  Author: David M. Anderson
  Built with AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic)
-->
<assembly xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1" manifestVersion="1.0">
  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings>
      <dpiAware xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2005/WindowsSettings">true/pm</dpiAware>
      <dpiAwareness xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">PerMonitorV2</dpiAwareness>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>
</assembly>
